<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sharuhen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sharuhen was a fortified town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, near Gaza, which served as a refuge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> after their expulsion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. According to the tomb biography of the soldier Ahmose son of Ebana, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a> besieged it for three years before taking it, around 1550–1540 BCE. Archaeologists have identified it with Tell el-Ajjul or Tell el-Far'ah South.</p></div>
